{
 "id": "w-ftc2",
 "course": "Calculus 1",
 "unit": "Integration",
 "title": "The Fundamental Theorem, Part 2, and Net Change",
 "subtitle": "Exact areas from antiderivatives, and total change from a rate.",
 "version": 1,
 "graphs": {},
 "lesson": {
  "slides": [
   {
    "id": "L1",
    "type": "mc",
    "text": "**The Fundamental Theorem, Part 2:** if $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$, then\n$$int[a,b] f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)$$\nNo more rectangles! For $int[0,2] x^2 dx$, which $F$ can you use?",
    "options": [
     "$F(x) = frac{x^3}{3}$",
     "$F(x) = 2x$",
     "$F(x) = x^2$",
     "$F(x) = frac{x^2}{2}$"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "hint": "We need $F' = x^2$.",
    "explain": "$frac{x^3}{3}$ (or $frac{x^3}{3} + C$; the $C$ cancels)."
   },
   {
    "id": "L2",
    "type": "num",
    "text": "So $int[0,2] x^2 dx = F(2) - F(0) = frac{8}{3} - 0$. Enter it.",
    "answer": 2.666667,
    "label": "integral =",
    "hint": "Use the fraction key, or 2.667.",
    "explain": "$frac{8}{3}$, exactly what the rectangles were closing in on."
   },
   {
    "id": "L3",
    "type": "mc",
    "text": "**Net change theorem:**\n$$int[a,b] F'(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)$$\nIntegrating a rate gives the total change. If $v(t)$ is velocity, $int[0,3] v(t) dt$ is the",
    "options": [
     "displacement (change in position)",
     "acceleration",
     "speed at $t = 3$",
     "average velocity"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "hint": "Velocity is the rate of change of position.",
    "explain": "Total change in position from $t = 0$ to $t = 3$."
   },
   {
    "id": "L4",
    "type": "steps",
    "text": "A particle moves with velocity $v(t) = t^2 - 4t$ (m/s) for $0 <= t <= 6$. It moves backward while $v < 0$, which is for $0 < t < 4$. The two pieces are\n$$int[0,4] v dt = -frac{32}{3} ;; int[4,6] v dt = frac{32}{3}$$",
    "steps": [
     {
      "id": "L4.1",
      "type": "num",
      "text": "**Displacement** is the net change: add the two pieces as they are.",
      "answer": 0,
      "label": "displacement =",
      "hint": "$-frac{32}{3} + frac{32}{3}$",
      "work": "displacement $= 0$ m"
     },
     {
      "id": "L4.2",
      "type": "num",
      "text": "**Total distance** counts both pieces as positive: add their sizes.",
      "answer": 21.333333,
      "label": "distance =",
      "hint": "$frac{32}{3} + frac{32}{3}$",
      "work": "distance $= frac{64}{3}$ m",
      "tol": 0.01
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L5",
    "type": "num",
    "text": "Water flows into a tank at $r(t) = 3t^2$ liters per minute. How much water flows in during the first 2 minutes?",
    "answer": 8,
    "label": "liters =",
    "hint": "$int[0,2] 3t^2 dt$",
    "explain": "$t^3$ from 0 to 2: 8 liters."
   },
   {
    "id": "L6",
    "type": "num",
    "text": "The **average value** of $f$ on $[a, b]$ is $frac{1}{b - a} int[a,b] f(x) dx$. Find the average of $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ on $[0, 1]$.",
    "answer": 2.333333,
    "label": "average =",
    "hint": "$int[0,1] (x^2 + 2) dx = frac{1}{3} + 2$, and $b - a = 1$.",
    "explain": "$frac{7}{3}$"
   }
  ]
 },
 "drills": {
  "theory": {
   "title": "Theory",
   "mode": "all",
   "questions": [
    {
     "id": "T1",
     "type": "match",
     "text": "Match each part of the Fundamental Theorem.",
     "pairs": [
      [
       "Part 1",
       "$frac{d}{dx} int[a,x] f(t) dt = f(x)$"
      ],
      [
       "Part 2",
       "$int[a,b] f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)$"
      ]
     ],
     "explain": "Part 1: differentiating undoes integrating. Part 2: compute areas with antiderivatives."
    },
    {
     "id": "T2",
     "type": "tf",
     "text": "In $F(b) - F(a)$, it does not matter which antiderivative $F$ you use.",
     "answer": true,
     "explain": "$(F(b) + C) - (F(a) + C) = F(b) - F(a)$. The $C$ cancels."
    },
    {
     "id": "T3",
     "type": "mc",
     "text": "What is\n$$int[-pi,pi] sin(x) dx$$",
     "options": [
      "0",
